// Specialised: fog of war.
//
// Prose only, no `point=` or `gate=`. It is offered from the topic menu, in a
// practice world that has no fog for a pointer to point at, and played once on
// its own the first time someone enters a fog world (Game::updateDialogue).

:: anchor=left size=20 pad=26 enter=type speed=44 line=1.4

@Signals Officer
Fog of war. Some worlds are made with it, and in those nobody sees the whole
map. Not you, not them.
---
@Signals Officer
You see your own land, and one province past every border. Wherever your
§**armies**§ stand, and the land around them. The sea off your coasts, and as
far as your ships' §**radar**§ reaches.

And everything your §**allies**§ see -- and anyone you guarantee, or who
guarantees you. Their eyes are yours. Their allies' are not.
---
@Signals Officer
Everything else is a memory. Dark land is land nobody you trust has looked at
lately: what you see there is how it was when it was last seen, and the
province panel tells you which turn that was.

Darkest of all is land nobody has ever seen. Its owner is the one on the
starting map, and that may have changed long ago.
---
@Signals Officer
Strangers keep their books shut. Their profile says §**Unknown**§ for
population, income and expenses, unless they choose to publish them.

History, the timelapse and rewinding a turn are switched off in these worlds.
They replay the truth, and the truth is the one thing you do not get.
---
@Signals Officer
So you go and look. Open a foreign country's profile and you will find
§**Intelligence**§. Plant a §**spy network**§ and you see all their land for
six turns. Steal their §**dossier**§ and their figures are yours for as long.

Both cost money up front, both can fail, and a failure is noticed. Get caught
by someone good at catching and they will know whose agents they were.
---
@Signals Officer
Your own profile has the other half: §**counter-intelligence**§. Three levels,
each paid for every turn, each making foreign spies fail more often.

And in these worlds there is an §**Intelligence**§ research tab: radar,
coastwatchers, reconnaissance, codebreakers and better security.
---
@Signals Officer
The computer plays by the same rules: it decides on what it can see, the same
as you. So do the diplomats who write to you. When they say "by our last
reports", they mean it.
---
@Signals Officer
To play one: §**Settings**§, then §**Experimental**§, then
§**Fog of War worlds**§. Then a map's §**i**§ button, and fog on before you
start. It is chosen once, for the life of that world.

That is the fog.
